Title: The Letter in the Tent
Order: 11
Production Code: 1x11

INT. EXPEDITION CAMP - REUEL'S TENT - NIGHT

AMB: a lion calling somewhere on the plain

[[Professor Stone]] and [[Reuel Briggs]] talk late over pipes about Meroe's ancient wealth. [[Charlie Vance]] half-listens from his bedroll; [[Jim Titus]] sits apart, reading a letter by candlelight.

PROFESSOR STONE
Your own Afro-Americans are a branch of a civilization that had its zenith when Egypt was still unlettered sand, Doctor. The evidence is there, if the world cares to look at it.

Stone retires. Reuel makes notes in his journal — and a cool wind moves through the tent that has nothing to do with the desert night. A shadow falls across the doorway: a woman's shape, unmistakably meant.

Behind Reuel, unseen by him directly, [[Jim Titus]] unfolds a letter from an inner pocket. The shadow's finger traces the page, letter by letter, and Reuel reads it as clearly as if it were his own.

TITUS'S LETTER (V.O.)
(Aubrey's handwriting)
Use your discretion about the final act. Be sure the letters are destroyed. Address me at Laurel Hill — Thomas Johnson. A.L.

The vision passes. Reuel sits frozen, the leopard's slowness to be helped suddenly making an entirely different kind of sense.

Later, a scream tears him out of a doze — Dianthe's voice, impossibly close, impossibly far away.

REUEL BRIGGS
Charlie — Charlie, wake up and listen!

They both hear it now: a woman's voice, calling his name across an ocean. Charlie mesmerizes him to draw out what second sight he can manage. Reuel, rigid, sees fragments of home — Molly weeping, a funeral — and, unmistakably, Aubrey's face.

REUEL BRIGGS (CONT'D)
(in trance)
Aubrey. Traitor. False friend. I will return for this.
